THE GREAT PLUSH MIGRATION

Length: 25 seconds
Aspect: 9:16
FPS: 30
Resolution: Ultra HD

OPENING RULE

Begin already in motion over the Golden Gate Bridge.
No setup delay. No title card. No end card.
The world should feel like a real prestige wildlife documentary — except the birds are clearly ridiculous plush creatures.

GLOBAL LOOK / ART STYLE

Nature-documentary cinematography with elegant long-lens telephoto shots, shallow depth of field, crisp coastal sunlight, soft atmospheric haze over San Francisco Bay.

All birds are handcrafted plush birds:
crimson fuzzy fabric, stitched seams, visible thread, uneven stuffing, floppy wings, googly eyes, cloth beaks, slightly worn texture.

The comedy comes from serious presentation, not from cheap cartoon behavior.

PHYSICS / MOTION RULES

All birds are always in active flight or landing motion.
No stiff toy poses.
Wings flap with soft cloth drag and slight asymmetry.
Stuffing wobbles subtly during turbulence.
Loose threads flutter in the slipstream.
Googly eyes jiggle independently during hard flaps, impacts, and turns.
Flight should feel clumsy but physically believable within the plush world.

SOUND DESIGN

Soft orchestral wildlife-documentary score with sweeping strings and light woodwinds.
Add subtle wind over the bay, soft fabric flaps, plush wing whooshes, tiny midair bumps, occasional stuffing “pffft” sounds when collisions or gusts happen.
Tone is majestic, sincere, lightly absurd.

NARRATION

Delivered with solemn documentary gravitas, natural pace:

“Each summer, the elusive Crimson Plushbird undertake its improbably migration to the San Francisco Bay…
 a journey of courage, confusion… and questionable aerodynamics..”
Some barely make the journey… powered only by hope and polyester stuffing.
Others are distracted by courtship, hunger… or basic confusion.
Survival demands resilience… and exceptional stitching.
And yet, the migration continues.”

SCENE STRUCTURE
(0.0–0.3s) SCENE 1 — OPENING CROSSING
Begin in a tight telephoto shot with plushbirds already flying across frame in front of the Golden Gate Bridge.

SCENE STRUCTURE
(0.3–3.5s) SCENE 1 — OPENING CROSSING
One crimson plushbird passes close to lens, slightly out of control, wings bending under wind resistance.
Bridge tower and cables sit in soft bokeh behind.
Loose threads stream in the wind.
Narration begins:

“Each summer, the elusive Crimson Plushbird undertake its improbably migration to the San Francisco Bay…”

(3.5–6.5s) SCENE 2 — THE STRUGGLER

Cut to one exhausted plushbird flying low over the water, wobbling, losing altitude, then flapping frantically to recover.
Its stuffing shifts from side to side. One googly eye points slightly wrong.
The bird just barely clears a swell of wind.
Narration:
“a journey of courage, confusion… and questionable aerodynamics..”

(6.5–9.5s) SCENE 3 — COURTSHIP CHAOS

Two male plushbirds break from formation near the bridge cables and perform an absurd mating display mid-air: over-flapping, circling, colliding softly, then correcting.
A tiny puff of stuffing escapes from one side seam.
One bird gets briefly twisted by a crosswind, then rejoins the airspace with dignity fully compromised.
SFX: small fabric “fwip-fwip.”

(9.5–12.5s) SCENE 4 — URBAN FORAGING

A plushbird swoops toward the shoreline rocks and rises again holding a tiny plush fish in its cloth beak.
The fish is also stitched, soft, and wide-eyed.
Another bird tries to steal it mid-flight and both nearly stall.
Narration:
“Some barely make the journey… powered only by hope and polyester stuffing.”

(12.5–16.5s) SCENE 5 — THE GRAND FLOCK

Wide aerial telephoto shot of dozens of crimson plushbirds forming a majestic V-formation over the Bay with the full Golden Gate spanning behind them.
For one beautiful second it looks genuinely noble.
Then the formation wobbles, bends unevenly, and one whole side drifts off-course as several birds flap at mismatched tempos.
Narration:
“…or basic confusion.”

(16.5–20.5s) SCENE 6 — WIND THROUGH THE CABLES

A strong gust tunnels through the bridge structure and hits the flock.
Several birds tumble softly, rotate, get briefly inverted, then recover with frantic but fluffy effort.
Threads whip in the air, wings overcorrect, eyes jiggle violently.
A couple of birds bonk together and bounce apart without harm.
Music swells slightly.

(20.5–23.2s) SCENE 7 — FINAL PUSH

Medium-long shot of the lead plushbird pushing forward into the wind, now framed heroically against open blue sky and bridge cables.
Its flapping is awkward but determined.
Other birds follow behind in scattered formation.
Narration:
“Survival demands resilience… and exceptional stitching.”


(23.2–25.0s) SCENE 8 — FINAL BUTTON

Close-up of the lead plushbird turning toward camera while still hovering awkwardly in place.
One googly eye settles a fraction later than the other.
It gives a tiny involuntary wobble as if trying to look noble and failing.
Narration:
“And yet, the migration continues.”

Hard cut out.
No text. No end card.